```matlab
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
gaussian_filter = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Standard deviation of 3 pixels
% Define Sobel operator in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, smoothed image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image); title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(gaussian_filter); title('Gaussian-Filtered Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image); title('Edge Image');
```
This modified code first reads the input image and converts it to grayscale as before. It then applies a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function with a standard deviation of 3 pixels (you can adjust this value for different levels of smoothing). The Sobel operators are applied to the smoothed image instead of the original image. Finally, the edge image is displayed along with the original image and the smoothed image for comparison.

