 I'd be happy to help you create a React component for converting temperatures between different units! Here's an example using the `useState` and `useEffect` hooks:

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  // Set up initial state with Fahrenheit as default unit
  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState(72);
  const [unit, setUnit] = useState('F');

  // Define conversions between Fahrenheit and Celsius
  const celsius = (f) => (5 / 9) * (f - 32);
  const fahrenheit = (c) => (c * 9 / 5) + 32;

  // Convert temperature when unit changes
  useEffect(() => {
    if (unit === 'C') {
      setTemperature(celsius(temperature));
    } else {
      setTemperature(fahrenheit(temperature));
    }
  }, [unit]);

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="temperature-input">Enter temperature:</label>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="temperature-input"
        value={temperature}
        onChange={(e) => setTemperature(parseFloat(e.target.value))}
      />
      <button onClick={() => setUnit('C')}>Convert to Celsius</button>
      <button onClick={() => setUnit('F')}>Convert to Fahrenheit</button>
      <p>Temperature in {unit}: {temperature}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

This component uses two pieces of state: the temperature value and the selected unit ('F' or 'C'). The `useEffect` hook is used to update the temperature value whenever the unit changes.

The conversions between Fahrenheit and Celsius are defined as separate functions, and the component uses these functions to calculate the converted temperature based on the user input and the selected unit.

