 To auto-prefix a string before using it as a regular expression in JavaScript, you can simply concatenate the prefix string with the regex pattern. Here's an example:

```javascript
let prefix = "auto-"; // Define your prefix here
let regexPattern = /yourRegexPattern/; // Define your regex pattern here

// Auto-prefix the regex pattern
let autoPrefixRegexPattern = new RegExp(`^${prefix}${regexPattern.source}`);

// Usage
let inputString = "some input string";
if (autoPrefixRegexPattern.test(inputString)) {
  console.log("Match found!");
} else {
  console.log("No match.");
}
```

In this example, we define a `prefix` variable and a `regexPattern` variable. We then create a new `RegExp` object called `autoPrefixRegexPattern`, which includes both the prefix and the original regex pattern. The `^` symbol at the beginning of the regex pattern is used to ensure that the entire string must start with the prefix.

