 Certainly! Here's an example of natural selection simulated using MATLAB, based on the Russell-Miller model. This model describes the change in frequency of alleles for a single locus over generations, taking into account mutation, selection, and drift.

```MATLAB
% Set initial parameters
N = 100; % population size
p_initial = 0.7; % initial frequency of favorable allele (Brown rabbit)
q_initial = 0.3; % initial frequency of unfavorable allele (White rabbit)
mu = 0.01; % mutation rate per gene per generation
s = 0.5; % selection coefficient (survival advantage of favorable allele)
generations = 10; % number of generations to simulate

% Initialize arrays to store frequencies
p = zeros(1, generations);
q = zeros(1, generations);

% Simulation loop
for i = 1:generations
    % Calculate probabilities of producing offspring based on fitness
    w = p.^(2 * s) .* (1 - p).^(2 * q); % fitnesses of individuals
    total_w = sum(w); % normalize fitnesses
    w = w ./ total_w; % probabilities of producing offspring

    % Create offspring by sampling from parents based on their probabilities
    offspring = rand(size(w)) < cumsum(w);

    % Calculate new frequencies of favorable and unfavorable alleles
    p(i+1) = sum(offspring .* (ones(1, N) == 1) .* (ones(1, N) == 1));
    q(i+1) = sum(offspring .* (ones(1, N) == 1) .* (ones(1, N) == 0));

    % Apply mutation
    p(i+1) = p(i+1) + mu * N * (q(i) + 2 * p(i) - p(i)^2);
end

% Plot results
plot(1:generations, p);
xlabel('Generations');
ylabel('Frequency of Favorable Allele');

