 Certainly! Here's an example PHP code snippet that utilizes MySQL queries with `GROUP BY` and `HAVING` clauses to help the coffee shop proprietor identify loyal customers based on their purchasing patterns:

```php
<?php
// Connect to the database using PDO
$pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=coffee_shop', 'username', 'password');

// Set query parameters
$product = 'americano'; // Replace with desired product name
$week_start = date('Y-m-d', strtotime('last Monday')); // Start of current week
$week_end = date('Y-m-d', strtotime('next Sunday')); // End of current week

// Prepare and execute SQL query
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("
    SELECT
        customers.name,
        COUNT(*) as purchase_count
    FROM
        transactions
    JOIN customers ON transactions.customer_id = customers.id
    WHERE
        product = :product AND
        transaction_date BETWEEN :week_start AND :week_end
    GROUP BY
        customers.id
    HAVING
        purchase_count >= 3
");
$stmt->bindParam(':product', $product);
$stmt->bindParam(':week_start', $week_start);
$stmt->bindParam(':week_end', $week_end);
$stmt->execute();

// Fetch results
$loyal_customers = [];
while ($row = $stmt->fetch()) {
    $loyal_customers[] = [
        'name' => $row['name'],
        'purchase_count' => $row['purchase_count']
    ];
}

// Print results
if (!empty($loyal_customers)) {
    echo "Loyal Customers:\n";
    print_r($loyal_customers);
} else {
    echo "No customers found.";
}
?>
```

