 To make the given code more efficient, here are some suggestions:

1. Memoize the sorting of prompts: Since the sorting operation doesn't change every time, memoize it to avoid unnecessary recomputations.

```javascript
const sortedPrompts = React.useMemo(() => {
  if (!prompts.data) return [];
  return prompts.data.sort((a, b) => b.updatedAt.getTime() - a.updatedAt.getTime());
}, [prompts.data]);
```

2. Use `useCallback` for event handlers: By wrapping event handlers with `useCallback`, they won't be recreated on every render, which can help improve performance.

```javascript
const handleEditPrompt = useCallback((p) => {
  setSelectedTargetPrompt(p.id);
  setEditedPrompt(p.prompt);
  setEditedName(p.name);
  onOpen();
}, [onOpen]);

const handleDelete = useCallback(() => {
  void deletePrompt.mutateAsync({ id: selectedTargetPrompt });
  onClose();
}, [deletePrompt, onClose, selectedTargetPrompt]);

const handleSave = useCallback(() => {
  void upsertPrompt.mutateAsync({
    id: selectedTargetPrompt,
    name: editedName,
    prompt: editedPrompt,
  });
  onClose();
}, [editedName, editedPrompt, onClose, selectTargetPrompt, upsertPrompt]);
```

3. Use `useEffect` to update query data when a prompt is deleted or updated: Instead of manually calling `utils.prompt.invalidate()`, use `useEffect` to listen for mutations and automatically update the query data.

```javascript
React.useEffect(() => {
  if (deletePrompt.isSuccess || upsertPrompt.isSuccess) {
    utils.prompt.invalidate();
  }
}, [deletePrompt.isSuccess, upsertPrompt.isSuccess]);
```

