 It looks like your `generateRandomPlanets` function is generating planets with random positions within a certain range (-30 to 30 for each axis), but it doesn't check if the newly generated planet is too close to the existing ones before adding it to the list. This can result in planets being placed too close to each other.

To solve this issue, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function to keep checking if the new planet is too close to any existing planet until a valid position is found. Here's an updated version of the function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
 const planets = [];
 let attempts = 0;

 while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
   attempts++;

   const planet = {
     position: [
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
       randomPosition(-30, 30),
     ],
     size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
     color: randomColor(),
     ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
     ringColor: randomColor(),
     ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
   };

   let tooClose = false;

   for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
     const existingPlanet = planets[i];

     for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
       const distance = Math.abs(planet.position[j] - existingPlanet.position[j]);

       if (distance < minDistance) {
         tooClose = true;
         break;
       }
     }

     if (!tooClose) {
       planets.push(planet);
       break;
     }
   }

   if (!tooClose) {
     break;
   }
 }

 return planets;
}
```

This updated version checks the distance between the newly generated planet and all existing planets in each dimension, and only adds the new planet to the list if none of them are closer than the minimum distance. This should help ensure that the planets are evenly spaced out.

