Quickselect works by selecting a pivot element and partitioning the array around it such that all elements smaller than the pivot come before it and all elements greater than the pivot come after it. We recursively apply this process on the sub-array where we want our answer (i.e., the lower half if k is smaller than the length of the array or the upper half otherwise) until we reach the sub-array containing the kth largest element.

Alternatively, using Heapsort, we first sort the list in O(n log n) time. Then, since the last k elements will be the k largest elements in the sorted list, we simply return the kth index's value from the end of the list, which takes O(1) additional time.

Both algorithms have their advantages and disadvantages. Quickselect has a better average time complexity than Heapsort but may take longer for worst cases. On the other hand, Heapsort always sorts the entire list and might not be the most efficient choice when only one element is required.

